/**
 * \defgroup distrib_student_t1 Student t, scale parametrization
 * \brief Univariate Student t distribution parametrized by location, scale, and degrees of freedom.
 *
 * Defined in `<laplace/distrib/student_t1.hpp>` as
 * \ref laplace::distrib::student_t1_distrib "student_t1_distrib", this distribution implements
 * log-density, cumulative, and complementary cumulative evaluators with mixed parameter and
 * observation derivatives up to order \f$6\f$, expected Fisher information with derivatives up to
 * order \f$4\f$, support queries, and robust sample starting values. Every evaluator is marked
 * `LAPLACE_HD` so host and Kokkos device code run identically; see \ref distrib_core for common
 * interface and tensor conventions, \ref distrib_generic for generic quantiles and random
 * generation, and \ref distrib_start for sample initialization.
 *
 * ## Classic form and parametrization
 *
 * The classic standardized Student t density (Student, 1908) with \f$\nu > 0\f$ degrees of
 * freedom is
 * \f[
 * t_\nu(z) = \frac{\Gamma((\nu + 1)/2)}{\sqrt{\nu\pi}\,\Gamma(\nu/2)}
 * \left(1 + \frac{z^2}{\nu}\right)^{-(\nu + 1)/2}, \qquad z \in \mathbb{R},
 * \f]
 * where \f$\Gamma\f$ is the gamma function and \f$z\f$ is the standardized variate. Substituting
 * \f$z = (y - \mu)/\sigma\f$ with location \f$\mu \in \mathbb{R}\f$ and scale \f$\sigma > 0\f$
 * gives the density \f$f(y) = t_\nu(z)/\sigma\f$ and the log-density
 * \f[
 * \ell(\mu, \sigma, \nu; y) = c(\nu) - \log\sigma - \frac{\nu + 1}{2}\log\!\left(1 + \frac{z^2}{\nu}\right),
 * \qquad c(\nu) = \log\frac{\Gamma((\nu + 1)/2)}{\Gamma(\nu/2)} - \frac{1}{2}\log(\nu\pi).
 * \f]
 * Here \f$\sigma\f$ is the scale rather than the standard deviation, which exists only for
 * \f$\nu > 2\f$ and equals \f$\sigma\sqrt{\nu/(\nu - 2)}\f$. Setting \f$\nu = 1\f$ yields the
 * Cauchy family (\ref distrib_cauchy "cauchy"), and \f$\nu \to \infty\f$ converges to the Gaussian
 * family (\ref distrib_gaussian1 "gaussian1"). All regularity descriptors are `infinitely_smooth`,
 * and `param_kinks()` and `y_kinks()` return no kinks.
 *
 * ## Log-density and Fisher information
 *
 * To avoid cancelling large gamma terms when \f$\nu \ge 8\f$, \f$c(\nu)\f$ calls the log-gamma
 * ratio helper in \ref student, and its derivatives in \f$\nu\f$ come from the half-step
 * polygamma difference helper in \ref gamma. With \f$r = z^2 / (\nu + z^2)\f$ and
 * \f$q = 1 - r = \nu / (\nu + z^2)\f$, pure \f$\nu\f$-derivatives of the shape term
 * \f$-\frac{\nu + 1}{2}\log(1 + z^2/\nu)\f$ follow the closed formula
 * \f[
 * D_{c, 0} = \frac{1}{2}(-1)^m \left[(m - 1)!\,\nu^{-m} B_m(r) + m!\,\nu^{-(m+1)} r \sum_{j=0}^{m} q^j\right],
 * \qquad m = c - 1 \ge 1,
 * \f]
 * where \f$B_m(r) = (1 + m r)(1 - r)^m - 1\f$ avoids cancellation near \f$r = 0\f$ via
 * \f$\operatorname{expm1}\f$. Mixed partials in \f$(\mu, \sigma, \nu)\f$ combine the
 * shape derivatives in \f$(z, \nu)\f$ with the \f$c(\nu)\f$ terms through the shared
 * location-scale derivative assembly, and `logpdf_dy_dk` uses \f$\partial/\partial y = -\partial/\partial\mu\f$.
 *
 * For the \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ components of `logpdf_dk` that carry no \f$\nu\f$-derivative, the
 * \f$z\f$-polynomials of the location-scale assembly grow like \f$z^{K-1}\f$ in the far tail
 * while the component they build is \f$O(1)\f$ or smaller; at \f$|z| = 10^8\f$, order \f$6\f$
 * loses three digits of the tensor's largest component when formed from the \f$(z, \nu)\f$ table.
 * When \f$|u| \ge w\f$ (equivalently \f$|z| \ge \sqrt{\nu}\f$), with \f$u = y - \mu\f$ and
 * \f$w = \sigma\sqrt{\nu}\f$, writing \f$\log(1 + z^2/\nu) = \log(u^2 + w^2) - \log(\nu\sigma^2)\f$
 * and \f$\log(u^2 + w^2) = 2\operatorname{Re}\log(u + i w)\f$ rewrites the log-density as
 * \f[
 * \ell(\mu, \sigma, \nu; y) = c(\nu) + \frac{\nu + 1}{2}\log\nu + \nu\log\sigma - (\nu + 1)\operatorname{Re}\log(u + i w).
 * \f]
 * Since \f$\partial/\partial\mu = -\partial/\partial u\f$,
 * \f$\partial/\partial\sigma = \sqrt{\nu}\,\partial/\partial w\f$, and
 * \f$\partial^{a+b}/(\partial u^a\,\partial w^b)\log(u + i w) = i^b (-1)^{K-1} (K - 1)!\,(u + i w)^{-K}\f$
 * for \f$K = a + b \ge 1\f$, the components of the block are
 * \f[
 * \frac{\partial^K \ell}{\partial\mu^a\,\partial\sigma^b}
 * = -(\nu + 1)(-1)^a \nu^{b/2} (-1)^{K-1} (K - 1)!\,\operatorname{Re}\!\left[i^b (u + i w)^{-K}\right]
 *   + [a = 0]\,\nu(-1)^{b-1}(b - 1)!\,\sigma^{-b},
 * \f]
 * where \f$[a = 0]\f$ equals \f$1\f$ for \f$a = 0\f$ and \f$0\f$ otherwise, and the last term is
 * all that \f$\nu\log\sigma\f$ contributes. One complex power per order is taken from the
 * reciprocal of \f$u + i w\f$ by repeated multiplication and scaled by \f$\max(|u|, w)\f$ so
 * that neither \f$u^2 + w^2\f$ nor the power overflows; no quantity on the right is a
 * difference of larger ones.
 *
 * The split is on \f$|u| \ge w\f$ rather than on \f$|z|\f$ alone or on \f$\nu\f$: below it,
 * \f$\operatorname{Re}[i^b (u + i w)^{-K}]\f$ is a small difference and \f$\nu\log\sigma\f$ is a
 * large term against a small result, reaching \f$10^{12}\f$ ulp at \f$\nu = 10^{12}\f$. Across
 * the reference grid, for \f$|z|/\sqrt{\nu} < 1\f$ the table route stays at most \f$33\f$ ulp
 * at order \f$6\f$ while the closed form reaches \f$1.5 \times 10^{12}\f$; for
 * \f$|z|/\sqrt{\nu} \ge 1\f$ the table route reaches \f$2.4 \times 10^3\f$ ulp while the closed
 * form stays at or below \f$4.6\f$ ulp. Script `tools/reference/distrib_student_t.py` checks the
 * rewrite and the closed component against SymPy (Meurer and others, 2017) at two points.
 *
 * The expected Fisher information matrix in \f$(\mu, \sigma, \nu)\f$ is block-diagonal with
 * entries \f$I_{\mu\mu} = (\nu + 1)/((\nu + 3)\sigma^2)\f$,
 * \f$I_{\sigma\sigma} = 2\nu/((\nu + 3)\sigma^2)\f$,
 * \f$I_{\sigma\nu} = -2/((\nu + 1)(\nu + 3)\sigma)\f$, and
 * \f$I_{\nu\nu} = \frac{1}{4}[\psi'(\nu/2) - \psi'((\nu + 1)/2)] - (\nu + 5)/(2\nu(\nu + 1)(\nu + 3))\f$.
 * Derivatives in \f$(\sigma, \nu)\f$ up to order \f$4\f$ combine inverse powers of \f$\sigma\f$,
 * partial fractions in \f$\nu\f$, and the polygamma difference helper in \ref gamma, which keeps
 * accuracy past the \f$\nu = 18\f$ series cutoff where \f$I_{\nu\nu} \sim 7/(2\nu^4)\f$.
 *
 * ## Cumulative functions and accuracy
 *
 * Cumulative probabilities delegate to `log_student_cdf` in \ref student, and their derivatives
 * in \f$(z, \nu)\f$ up to order \f$6\f$ from \ref student (backed by \ref incomplete_beta) are
 * pushed to \f$(\mu, \sigma, \nu)\f$. On the heavy side (\f$z \ge 0\f$ for `log_cdf`,
 * \f$z \le 0\f$ for `log_ccdf`), a Bell polynomial recursion in the opposite log-tail \f$l\f$
 * computes \f$\log(1 - e^l)\f$ and its derivatives without cancellation (see \ref chain_pair).
 *
 * Measured against SymPy (Meurer and others, 2017) at \f$400\f$ and \f$600\f$ bits for
 * `logpdf_dk`, and against `mpmath` (The mpmath development team, 2023) at \f$400\f$/\f$600\f$ bits
 * for `expected_info_dk` and \f$280\f$/\f$360\f$ bits for cumulative derivatives, worst-case
 * errors across CPU `gcc` and CUDA builds (see \ref accuracy and `docs/numerics.md`) stay within:
 *
 * | Method | Worst scaled ulp (orders \f$1\f$..\f$6\f$ or \f$0\f$..\f$4\f$) | Test bound |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | `logpdf_dk` | \f$3.68, 2.35, 3.66, 5.1, 20.1, 107\f$ | \f$6, 4, 6, 8, 31, 161\f$ |
 * | `log_cdf_dk` | \f$1.2 \times 10^3, 962, 786, 1.23 \times 10^3, 977, 6450\f$ | \f$1800, 1450, 1180, 1850, 1470, 9700\f$ |
 * | `log_ccdf_dk` | \f$1.2 \times 10^3, 962, 786, 1.23 \times 10^3, 977, 805\f$ | \f$1800, 1450, 1180, 1850, 1470, 1210\f$ |
 * | `expected_info_dk` | \f$161, 56, 51, 35, 17\f$ | \f$240, 85, 76, 53, 26\f$ |
 *
 * Here Fisher information errors are measured in ulp of each component, peaking at \f$I_{\nu\nu}\f$
 * for \f$\nu = 17.9\f$. The worst density errors sit on the components with at least one
 * \f$\nu\f$-derivative, which the far-tail closed form does not cover, and peak at \f$107\f$ ulp
 * at order \f$6\f$ at \f$z = 10^8\f$, \f$\nu = 2\f$. For
 * `log_cdf_dk` and `log_ccdf_dk`, orders \f$1\f$, \f$2\f$, \f$4\f$ and \f$5\f$ peak at
 * \f$|z| = 30\f$, \f$\nu = 10^4\f$ and order \f$3\f$ at \f$|z| = 30\f$, \f$\nu = 10^8\f$, while
 * order \f$6\f$ peaks at \f$|z| = 31.6\f$, \f$\nu = 3.5\f$ for `log_cdf_dk` (\f$6450\f$ ulp) and
 * at \f$|z| = 4\f$, \f$\nu = 2\f$ for `log_ccdf_dk` (\f$805\f$ ulp). At \f$z = 30\f$,
 * \f$\nu = 10^4\f$, the error is the size
 * of the uncertainty the arguments carry: recomputed in `mpmath` at \f$280\f$ bits with one ulp of
 * a `double` added to one argument at a time, adding one ulp to \f$\sigma\f$ shifts orders
 * \f$1\f$ to \f$6\f$ by \f$1.51 \times 10^3, 1.21 \times 10^3, 970, 1.54 \times 10^3, 1.23 \times 10^3\f$,
 * and \f$968\f$ ulp of each order's scale, adding one ulp to \f$y\f$ shifts them by
 * \f$809, 649, 519, 828, 657\f$, and \f$520\f$, and adding one ulp to \f$\mu\f$ or \f$\nu\f$
 * moves them by \f$16\f$ to \f$28\f$. At \f$\nu = 1\f$ the family matches `cauchy_distrib`
 * within \f$9 \times 10^{-14}\f$ on density and \f$3.2 \times 10^{-12}\f$ on `log_cdf` for
 * \f$|z| < 20\f$, while at \f$\nu = 10^8\f$ and \f$10^{12}\f$ the differences from
 * `gaussian1_distrib` are \f$0.5\f$, \f$0.25\f$ and \f$0.25\f$ of the \f$O(1/\nu)\f$ scale of the
 * limit and are the same at both, so no digit is lost as \f$\nu\f$ grows.
 * Quadrature, score-variance, and CUDA host/device checks agree
 * within \f$1.1 \times 10^{-15}\f$, \f$7 \times 10^{-15}\f$, and \f$8.7 \times 10^{-15}\f$.
 * At small integer \f$\nu \le 6\f$, the far-tail \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ block of `log_cdf_dk`
 * loses digits on its own tiny entries while the full tensor remains bounded above.
 *
 * ## References
 *
 * - [1] Student (1908), The probable error of a mean, Biometrika, 6(1), 1-25. https://doi.org/10.2307/2331554
 * - [2] A. Meurer and others (2017), SymPy: symbolic computing in Python, PeerJ Computer Science, 3, e103. https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.103
 * - [3] The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
 */

/**
 * \defgroup distrib_student_t2 Student t, standard deviation
 * \brief Univariate Student t distribution parametrized by location, standard deviation, and degrees of freedom.
 *
 * Defined in `<laplace/distrib/student_t2.hpp>` as
 * \ref laplace::distrib::student_t2_distrib "student_t2_distrib", this distribution reparametrizes
 * \ref distrib_student_t1 "student_t1" by its standard deviation \f$\sigma > 0\f$ for \f$\nu > 2\f$.
 * It provides the full `LAPLACE_HD` interface up to derivative order \f$6\f$ for log-density and
 * cumulative tails and order \f$4\f$ for expected Fisher information, together with generic
 * quantiles (\ref distrib_generic) and moment-based starting values (\ref distrib_start).
 *
 * ## Classic form and parametrization
 *
 * Starting from the standardized Student t density \f$t_\nu(z)\f$ (Student, 1908), finite
 * variance requires \f$\nu > 2\f$, in which case the standard deviation \f$\sigma > 0\f$ is
 * related to the scale \f$s\f$ of `student_t1_distrib` by \f$s = \sigma\sqrt{(\nu - 2)/\nu}\f$.
 * Setting \f$z = (y - \mu)/s\f$ with location \f$\mu \in \mathbb{R}\f$ gives the log-density
 * \f[
 * \ell(\mu, \sigma, \nu; y) = c(\nu) - \log s - \frac{\nu + 1}{2}\log\!\left(1 + \frac{z^2}{\nu}\right),
 * \qquad s = \sigma\sqrt{\frac{\nu - 2}{\nu}},
 * \f]
 * where \f$c(\nu)\f$ is the normalizing constant of `student_t1_distrib`. Unlike `student_t1`,
 * \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ directly estimates the mean and standard deviation, and the Gaussian limit
 * (\ref distrib_gaussian1 "gaussian1") matches both moments as \f$\nu \to \infty\f$. As
 * \f$\nu \to 2^+\f$, \f$s\f$ shrinks to zero and the distribution concentrates around \f$\mu\f$. All
 * regularity flags are `infinitely_smooth` and no kinks occur.
 *
 * ## Pushforward derivatives and Fisher information
 *
 * Value methods (`logpdf()`, `cdf()`, `ccdf()`, `log_cdf()`, `log_ccdf()`) delegate to
 * `as_t1()`, the equivalent `student_t1_distrib` at \f$(\mu, s, \nu)\f$. Derivative tensors up
 * to order \f$6\f$ (`logpdf_dk()` and `log_cdf_tail_dk()`) compose the tensors of
 * `student_t1_distrib` through \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_pair_dk "chain_rule_pair_dk()"
 * (\ref chain_pair "chain_pair") from \f$(\mu, s, \nu)\f$ to \f$(\mu, \sigma, \nu)\f$;
 * `student_t2_distrib` does not implement the far-tail \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ closed form itself,
 * and instead inherits it from `student_t1_distrib` (see \ref distrib_student_t1 "student_t1").
 * Writing \f$r(\nu) = \sqrt{1 - 2/\nu}\f$ so
 * \f$s = \sigma r(\nu)\f$, linearity in \f$\sigma\f$ reduces the chain rule to one-dimensional
 * derivatives \f$r^{(q)}\f$ computed recursively from \f$r^2 = 1 - 2/\nu\f$:
 * \f[
 * r^{(q)} = \frac{-2(-1)^q q!\,\nu^{-(q+1)} - \sum_{i=1}^{q-1} \binom{q}{i} r^{(i)} r^{(q-i)}}{2r}.
 * \f]
 * Mixed \f$y\f$-derivatives (`logpdf_dy_dk()`) use translation invariance
 * \f$\partial/\partial y = -\partial/\partial\mu\f$.
 *
 * Under the standard-deviation parametrization, the expected Fisher information in
 * \f$(\mu, \sigma, \nu)\f$ has entries
 * \f[
 * I_{\mu\mu} = \frac{1}{\sigma^2}\left(1 + \frac{6}{(\nu + 3)(\nu - 2)}\right),
 * \quad I_{\sigma\sigma} = \frac{2\nu}{(\nu + 3)\sigma^2},
 * \quad I_{\sigma\nu} = \frac{6}{(\nu + 3)(\nu - 2)(\nu + 1)\sigma},
 * \f]
 * together with
 * \f[
 * I_{\nu\nu} = I^{(1)}_{\nu\nu} + \frac{2}{(\nu + 3)\nu(\nu - 2)^2} - \frac{4}{\nu(\nu - 2)(\nu + 1)(\nu + 3)},
 * \f]
 * where \f$I^{(1)}_{\nu\nu}\f$ is the scale-parametrization component from \ref distrib_student_t1
 * "student_t1". Derivatives up to order \f$4\f$ follow from partial fractions in \f$\nu\f$,
 * \f$\nu - 2\f$, \f$\nu + 1\f$, and \f$\nu + 3\f$ combined with the derivatives of
 * \f$I^{(1)}_{\nu\nu}\f$.
 *
 * ## Accuracy and near-boundary behavior
 *
 * Measured against SymPy (Meurer and others, 2017) and `mpmath` (The mpmath development team,
 * 2023) references across CPU `gcc` and CUDA builds (see \ref accuracy and `docs/numerics.md`),
 * worst scaled ulp errors stay within:
 *
 * | Method | Worst scaled ulp (orders \f$1\f$..\f$6\f$ or \f$0\f$..\f$4\f$) | Test bound |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | `logpdf_dk` | \f$5.81, 6.87, 9.65, 103, 9.1, 31.2\f$ | \f$9, 11, 15, 155, 14, 47\f$ |
 * | `log_cdf_dk` (\f$\nu \ge 2.001\f$) | \f$903, 725, 580, 925, 733, 582\f$ | \f$1360, 1090, 870, 1390, 1100, 880\f$ |
 * | `log_ccdf_dk` (\f$\nu \ge 2.001\f$) | \f$903, 725, 580, 925, 733, 582\f$ | \f$1360, 1090, 870, 1390, 1100, 880\f$ |
 * | `log_cdf_dk` (\f$\nu = 2 + 10^{-6}\f$) | \f$17.9, 1.5 \times 10^5, 5.9 \times 10^6, 1.6 \times 10^7, 4.6 \times 10^7, 1.3 \times 10^8\f$ | \f$27, 2.2 \times 10^5, 8.8 \times 10^6, 2.4 \times 10^7, 6.9 \times 10^7, 1.95 \times 10^8\f$ |
 * | `expected_info_dk` | \f$321, 115, 100, 54, 35\f$ | \f$480, 173, 150, 82, 52\f$ |
 *
 * The \f$103\f$ ulp of `logpdf_dk` at order \f$4\f$ sit on a component with four
 * \f$\nu\f$-derivatives at \f$z = 4\f$, \f$\nu = 10\f$, where
 * \ref laplace::math::chain_rule_pair_dk "chain_rule_pair_dk()" combines the tensors of
 * `student_t1_distrib` with the derivatives of \f$r(\nu)\f$; that component is the least accurate
 * of the family's density tensors.
 * For `log_cdf_dk` and `log_ccdf_dk` with
 * \f$\nu \ge 2.001\f$, orders \f$1\f$ to \f$5\f$ peak at \f$z = 30\f$, \f$\nu = 10^4\f$, where the
 * error is the size of the uncertainty the arguments carry (see \ref distrib_student_t1
 * "student_t1"). When \f$\nu \to 2^+\f$ (\f$\nu < 2.001\f$), the derivatives \f$r^{(q)}\f$ grow rapidly and
 * \f$z = (y - \mu)/s\f$ becomes huge for \f$y \ne \mu\f$; at \f$\nu = 2 + 10^{-6}\f$, `logpdf_dk`
 * stays within \f$39\f$ ulp while order-\f$6\f$ `log_cdf_dk` retains about \f$8\f$ decimal digits
 * due to the intrinsic conditioning of the map \f$\nu \mapsto s\f$. Fisher information errors
 * peak at \f$\nu = 17.9\f$, and quadrature, score-variance, and CUDA host/device checks agree
 * within \f$1.1 \times 10^{-15}\f$, \f$7 \times 10^{-15}\f$, and \f$8.7 \times 10^{-15}\f$.
 *
 * ## References
 *
 * - [1] Student (1908), The probable error of a mean, Biometrika, 6(1), 1-25. https://doi.org/10.2307/2331554
 * - [2] A. Meurer and others (2017), SymPy: symbolic computing in Python, PeerJ Computer Science, 3, e103. https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.103
 * - [3] The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
 */
